This repo is Express **4.21.2**, and route matching lives in the `Router` internals:

## The answer: `Layer` class, `Layer.prototype.match` — `lib/router/layer.js`

**1. Regex construction — `Layer` constructor (line 45):**
```js
var pathRegexp = require('path-to-regexp');   // v0.1.12 (package.json line 54)
...
this.regexp = pathRegexp(path, this.keys = [], opts);
```
Each layer (registered route path) is compiled **once at registration time** via the legacy `path-to-regexp@0.x` API, which turns a path like `'/users/:id'` into a single `RegExp` and collects the named params into `this.keys`. The actual regex-building logic is in the external `path-to-regexp` dependency — Express's own source contains no regex construction of its own.

The constructor also sets two fast-path flags (lines 48–49):
```js
this.regexp.fast_star  = path === '*'
this.regexp.fast_slash = path === '/' && opts.end === false
```

**2. The match test — `Layer.prototype.match(path)` (lines 110–156):**
```js
Layer.prototype.match = function match(path) {
  ...
  // fast paths for fast_slash and fast_star (lines 113-126)
  ...
  match = this.regexp.exec(path)   // line 129 — the actual evaluation
  ...
  this.params = {};
  this.path = match[0]
  // lines 145-153: populate this.params from match captures + this.keys,
  // decoded via decode_param() (throws a 400 URIError on bad %XX encoding)
  return true;
};
```
The core evaluation is **`this.regexp.exec(path)` at line 129**. On a hit it stores the matched segment in `this.path` and the decoded named params in `this.params`; on a miss it clears both and returns `false`.

**3. Call site in the router — `lib/router/index.js`:**
The router's per-layer dispatch loop in `process_params` calls `matchLayer(layer, path)` (line 226), which is a thin try/catch wrapper (lines 583–589):
```js
function matchLayer(layer, path) {
  try {
    return layer.match(path);
  } catch (err) {
    return err;   // e.g. decode_param URIError → propagated as a 400
  }
}
```
After a successful match, `req.params` is set from `layer.params` (line ~271) and `layer.handle_request(...)` dispatches to the route handler.

**TL;DR:** `Layer` (lib/router/layer.js) — the regex is built in the constructor via `path-to-regexp` (line 45), and the match test is `Layer.prototype.match` (line 110), specifically `this.regexp.exec(path)` at line 129. (Note: Express 5 rewrote this with `path-to-regexp` v6+ and `router.use()` semantics; this codebase is still the 4.x design.)
